  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday morning through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 226 PM EST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nCool and Dry Weekend:\n\nA longwave mid-level trough will be parked over the Eastern US this \nweekend, with cool and dry northwest flow over Georgia. This will \nusher in the coolest airmass of the season thus far, with areas of \nnorth and west-central GA seeing their first freeze Saturday \nmorning. A Freeze Watch has been issued for these areas. After the \ncore of coolest air spreads over the area on Saturday, light winds \nand clear skies Saturday night will allow temperatures to fall to at \nor below freezing for nearly all of North and Central GA. Additional \nfreeze or frost headlines will be needed for this. For some context, \nthis first freeze will fall right in line with the average first \nfreeze time period of mid-November for our area. High temperatures \neach afternoon this weekend will generally be in the 50s under sunny \nskies.\n\nSlow Warming and Mostly Dry Next Week:\n\nFrom early to middle portions of next week, models are in good \nagreement on the longwave trough being kicked off to the northeast \nas another wave slides from the Southern Plains into the Southeast \nUS. At this time, it's not looking like this system will have much \nmoisture or punch to it, but could still bring a few showers to the \narea in the Tuesday timeframe. It will also moderate the \ntemperatures a bit, with lows climbing back above freezing, and \nhighs returning to the 60s. Following this, guidance continues to \nshow a large ridge building from the Gulf up across the Southeast US \nby late next week, which would keep conditions dry and bring warmer \ntemperatures back into the area.",
